== Chapter 6

#align(center)[#smallcaps(all: true)[THE NEED FOR SCRIPTURE, AS A GUIDE AND TEACHER, IN COMING TO GOD AS A CREATOR.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. God gives his elect a better help to the knowledge of himself—namely, the Holy Scriptures. He did this from the very first.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. First, by oracles and visions, and the ministry of the patriarchs. Second, by the giving of the Law and the preaching of the Prophets. Why the doctrines of religion were committed to writing.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. This view confirmed: 1. by the depravity of our nature, which makes it necessary for every one who would know God to turn to the word; 2. from those passages of the Psalms in which God is presented as reigning.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[4\. Another confirmation from certain direct statements in the Psalms. Lastly, from the words of our Savior.]

#v(0.8em)

1\. #smallcaps[Therefore], though the brightness that meets every eye, both in the heavens and on the earth, leaves the ingratitude of man without excuse—since God, to bring the whole human race under the same condemnation, holds out to all without exception a mirror of his Deity in his works—another and better help must be given to guide us rightly to God as Creator. It was not in vain, then, that he added the light of his Word, so as to make himself known for salvation, and granted this privilege to those whom he was pleased to bring into a nearer and closer relationship with himself. For, seeing how the minds of men were tossed to and fro and found no sure resting place, he chose the Jews as a people of his own, and then fenced them in so that they might not go astray like others. And it is not in vain that by the same means he keeps us in the pure knowledge of himself, since without this even those who seem to stand firm compared with others would soon fall away. For just as the old, or those whose sight is weak, when any book, however fine, is set before them, can see that something is written but can scarcely make out two words together, yet with the help of glasses begin to read distinctly, so Scripture gathers together the impressions of Deity that till then lay confused in our minds, scatters the darkness, and shows us the true God clearly. So God bestows a gift of singular value when, to instruct the Church, he uses not only mute teachers but opens his own sacred mouth; when he not only proclaims that some God must be worshiped, but at the same time declares that He is the God to whom worship is due; when he not only teaches his elect to look to God, but shows himself as the God to whom they should look.

From the very first, the course God followed toward his Church was to add his Word to these common proofs, as a surer and more direct mark for distinguishing him. And there can be no doubt that it was by this help that Adam, Noah, Abraham, and the other patriarchs reached that intimate knowledge which in a way set them apart from unbelievers. I am not yet speaking of the distinctive doctrines of faith by which they were enlightened to the hope of eternal life. To pass from death to life, they had to know God not only as Creator but also as Redeemer; and they certainly gained both kinds of knowledge from the Word. In order, however, the knowledge given first was the knowledge that made them acquainted with the God by whom the world was made and is governed. To this first knowledge was later added the more inward knowledge which alone gives life to dead souls, and by which God is known not only as the Creator of the world and the sole author and disposer of all that happens, but also as Redeemer, in the person of the Mediator. But since we have not yet come to the fall and the corruption of nature, I put off treating the remedy for now.#footnote[For which, see Book 2 c. 6 etc. (Beveridge’s note.)] Let the reader remember, then, that I am not yet treating the covenant by which God adopted the children of Abraham, or that part of doctrine by which, since it is founded in Christ, believers have, properly speaking, been set apart in all ages from the profane heathen. I am only showing that we must go to Scripture to learn the sure marks that distinguish God, as the Creator of the world, from the whole herd of invented gods. Later, in due course, we shall consider the work of Redemption. Meanwhile, though we shall bring forward many passages from the New Testament, and some also from the Law and the Prophets, in which Christ is expressly mentioned, their only purpose will be to show that God, the Maker of the world, is revealed to us in Scripture, and his true character explained, so as to save us from wandering up and down, as in a labyrinth, in search of some doubtful deity.

2\. Whether God revealed himself to the fathers by oracles and visions,#footnote[The French adds, “C’est à dire, temoignages celestes;”—that is to say, messages from heaven. (Beveridge’s note.)] or, through the agency and ministry of men, put into their minds what they were to hand down to their descendants, there can be no doubt that the certainty of what he taught them was firmly engraved on their hearts, so that they were sure and knew that what they had learned came from God. For God always accompanied his word with a sure testimony, infinitely above mere opinion. At length, so that the truth of doctrine might live on in the world in an unbroken course through all ages, he willed that the same oracles he had entrusted to the fathers should be committed, as it were, to public records. With this purpose the law was proclaimed, and prophets were afterward added to be its interpreters. For though the uses of the law were many, as will appear better in its proper place,#footnote[Book 2 c. 7 and 8. (Beveridge’s note.)] and the special task given to Moses and all the prophets was to teach the way of reconciliation between God and man (which is why Paul calls Christ “the end of the law,” Rom 10:4), still I repeat that, besides the distinctive doctrine of faith and repentance in which Christ is set forth as Mediator, the Scriptures use certain marks and tokens to distinguish the only true God, as the Creator and Governor of the world, and so guard against his being confused with the herd of false deities. Therefore, while it is right for man to use his eyes earnestly in considering the works of God, since he has been given a place in this most glorious theater to be a spectator of them, his special duty is to listen to the Word, so that he may profit the more.#footnote[Tertullian, Apologet. adv. Gentes: “Quae plenius et impressius tam ipsum quam dispositiones ejus et voluntates adiremus, instrumentum adjecit literaturæ,” &c. \[That we might come more fully and more deeply both to himself and to his ordinances and will, he added the instrument of writing.\] (Beveridge’s note.)] So it is no wonder that those who are born in darkness grow more and more hardened in their stupor, because the great majority, instead of keeping within due bounds by listening teachably to the Word, exult in their own empty notions. If true religion is to shine on us, our principle must be that we have to begin with heavenly teaching, and that no man can gain even the smallest portion of right and sound doctrine without being a disciple of Scripture. Hence the first step in true knowledge is taken when we reverently embrace the testimony which God has been pleased to give of himself there. For not only faith, full and perfect faith, but all right knowledge of God is born of obedience. And surely in this God has provided for mankind in all ages with singular Providence.

3\. For if we consider how prone the human mind is to slip into forgetfulness of God, how ready it is for every kind of error, how bent, again and again, on inventing new and fictitious religions, it will be easy to see how necessary it was to make such a sealed record of heavenly doctrine, so that it would not perish through the neglect, vanish amid the errors, or be corrupted by the presumptuous audacity of men. Since it is plain, then, that God, foreseeing that his image imprinted on the beautiful form of the universe would have too little effect, has given the help of his Word to all whom he has ever been pleased to instruct effectually, we too must keep to this straight path if we earnestly aspire to a genuine contemplation of God. We must go, I say, to the Word, where the character of God, drawn from his works, is described accurately and to the life, since these works are judged there not by our depraved judgment but by the standard of eternal truth. If, as I said a little earlier, we turn aside from it, however great our speed, we shall never reach the goal, because we are off the course. We should consider that the brightness of the divine countenance, which even an apostle calls inaccessible (1 Tim 6:16), is a kind of labyrinth—a labyrinth we cannot find our way out of unless the Word serves us as a thread to guide our path; and that it is better to limp along the way than to run with the greatest speed off it. Hence the Psalmist, when he teaches that superstition should be banished from the world so that pure religion may flourish, repeatedly (Ps 93, 96, 97, 99, etc.) presents God as #emph[reigning]\; by this word he means not the power which God possesses and exercises in governing all of nature, but the doctrine by which God asserts his rightful supremacy; for error can never be rooted out of the heart of man until the true knowledge of God has been planted in it.

4\. Accordingly, the same prophet, after saying that the heavens declare the glory of God, that the firmament proclaims the works of his hands, and that the regular succession of day and night proclaims his Majesty, goes on to speak of the Word: “The Law of the #smallcaps[Lord],” he says, “is perfect, reviving the soul; the testimony of the #smallcaps[Lord] is trustworthy, making wise the simple. The precepts of the #smallcaps[Lord] are right, bringing joy to the heart; the commandments of the #smallcaps[Lord] are radiant, giving light to the eyes” (Ps 19:1–9). For though he includes other uses of the law as well,#footnote[As to which, see Book 2 c. 7, sec. 6, 10, 12. (Beveridge’s note.)] his general meaning is that it is the proper school for training the children of God, since the invitation given to all nations to see him in the heavens and the earth is of no avail. The same point is made in the 29th Psalm, where the Psalmist, after speaking of the dreadful voice of God, which in thunder, wind, rain, whirlwind, and storm shakes the earth, makes the mountains tremble, and breaks the cedars, concludes by saying that “in His temple all cry, ‘Glory!’”—for unbelievers are deaf to all God’s words when they echo in the air. In the same way another psalm, after describing the raging waves of the sea, ends like this: “Your testimonies are fully confirmed; holiness adorns Your house for all the days to come” (Ps 93:5). To the same effect are our Savior’s words to the Samaritan woman, when he told her that her nation and all other nations worshiped what they did not know, and that the Jews alone worshiped the true God (John 4:22). Since the human mind, in its weakness, could not possibly come to God unless aided and upheld by his sacred word, it necessarily followed that all mankind except the Jews, because they sought God without the Word, were lost in futility and error.
